<!doctype html>
<!--
  The Knot Garden.

  Two bands of low clipped box woven over and under each other inside a square
  edging, and a block of one colour standing in each of the eight compartments
  the weave makes. It holds thirty-two plants where the Long Walk holds
  forty-eight, the Crossing twenty-four, the Orchard twenty and the Quiet Garden
  ten, all in the same square.

  **This is the first area whose rule reads a plant's colour.** The other four
  grade by height and nothing else. A knot garden cannot: its compartments are
  blocks of one colour, and a block of one colour is what makes an interlaced
  pattern legible from the side of it. So a colour picks which pair of opposite
  compartments a plant belongs to, and its height picks the place within one —
  symmetrical in colour, graded in height, which is what a knot garden is.

  **The eight compartments are four mirror pairs**, opposite across the middle
  of the plot, and a pair holds one colour. Nothing is reserved: a pair is
  claimed by the first plant to stand in it, and only plants of that colour may
  join it afterwards. The middle of the knot holds no plant, as the Crossing's
  paving does and for the same reason.

  **The weave is drawn rather than implied.** At each of the four crossings one
  band carries on through and swells where the two have grown into each other,
  and the other stops square against its face and starts again beyond it. Which
  does which alternates round the knot. That is also how a real knot garden is
  made — living hedge cannot be woven — so the thing that is right
  horticulturally is the thing that reads as a knot.

  **This page is the one the app talks to, not a picture of it.** The plants are
  grown here, in the browser, from the same SeedCore that grows them on the
  phone — `plant.js` loads that library built for wasm32 and `knot.js` hands it
  each planting the plot service holds. Nothing is drawn from an image and
  nothing is precomputed: a plant on this page is the plant on the phone because
  it is the same arithmetic, not because somebody kept two drawings in step. The
  hedging comes from the same place, through `Organic`.

  **The sky is the real one**, worked out for wherever this browser is keeping
  time and asking for nothing to find out — `sky.js`, a port of SeedCore's
  `Sky`, held to it by `tools/reference/check_sky.mjs`. Both halves of it: the
  plots float, so the stars under them are drawn too.

  **It is a night, so the page is dark whatever the reader's system says.**
  Every other page here follows `prefers-color-scheme`; this one cannot,
  because the garden is lit by its own sky and a pale page around it is a
  second room.

  `noindex`, for the reason `/g` gives: walkable and indexed are different
  properties and only one of them was asked for.

  Static, like the rest: no analytics, no font CDN, no third-party anything.
-->
<!-- `data-module` is stamped by index.php: the module's address with this
     build's mark on it, so a new one is a new address. -->
<html lang="en" data-module="PG_MODULE">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1, viewport-fit=cover" />
    <title>Peace Garden — the Knot Garden</title>
    <meta name="description" content="Low hedging woven over and under, and the plants two people grew filling its compartments." />
    <meta name="color-scheme" content="dark" />
    <meta name="robots" content="noindex" />
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/site.css" />
    <link rel="icon" href="/assets/icon.svg" type="image/svg+xml" />
    <link rel="apple-touch-icon" href="/assets/icon-180.png" />
  </head>

  <body class="night">
    <!--
      The plot itself, behind everything. The sky is behind it, and the pool of
      light sits between them so the plot stands in front of the sky rather than
      on top of a picture of one.
    -->
    <div class="walk" aria-hidden="true">
      <canvas id="sky"></canvas>
      <div class="pool"></div>
    </div>

    <main class="page walk-page" id="page">
      <!--
        The bar. On every page of the site, and here it says one more thing: the
        link back to the map carries this area in its fragment, and `/g` reads
        one — so coming back from an area lands in that area rather than at the
        top of a map the reader has to find their place in again. `gates.js`
        writes the fragment.

        **It does not name the area.** It did, in a `.label` beside the link,
        and the heading under the plot names it too — the same words twice on
        one screen, and the heading is the one that belongs to the drawing.
      -->
      <header class="masthead bar">
        <a class="bar__home" href="/">
          <img class="mark" src="/assets/icon.svg" alt="" width="44" height="44" />
          <span class="wordmark">Peace Garden</span>
        </a>
        <!--
          The ways on from here, as glyphs rather than words, so they read the
          same in every language: the garden (the app's own Garden mark,
          `GardenGlyph` in Chrome.swift), what the names mean (an open book,
          the app's `MeaningsGlyph`), and the language. Each glyph's words
          are its name and tooltip, written from the catalogue by the page's
          script. See site.css §The bar's glyphs.
        -->
        <span class="bar__ways">
          <a class="bar-glyph" href="/garden" id="bar-hub" data-s-label="gardenTitle"><svg class="bar-glyph__mark" viewBox="0 0 15 15" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M 4.674 4.059 C 3.796 1.407, 6.716 1.407, 5.838 4.059 C 8.089 2.405, 8.991 5.181, 6.198 5.166 C 8.467 6.795, 6.105 8.512, 5.256 5.850 C 4.407 8.512, 2.045 6.795, 4.314 5.166 C 1.521 5.181, 2.423 2.405, 4.674 4.059 Z M 5.784 14.100 C 5.652 10.932, 5.124 9.612, 5.256 7.500 M 10.140 14.100 C 9.678 10.932, 9.150 8.556, 8.622 6.180 M 10.140 14.100 C 10.338 10.404, 10.470 6.972, 10.338 3.540 M 10.140 14.100 C 10.800 10.932, 11.658 8.820, 12.318 6.972 M 4.200 14.100 L 11.328 14.100" vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke" /></svg></a>
          <a class="bar-glyph" href="/meanings" data-s-label="meaningsTitle"><svg class="bar-glyph__mark" viewBox="0 0 20 20" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M10 5.2C8.2 3.9 5.4 3.5 2.75 3.9V15.4C5.4 15 8.2 15.4 10 16.7C11.8 15.4 14.6 15 17.25 15.4V3.9C14.6 3.5 11.8 3.9 10 5.2ZM10 5.2V16.7" vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke" /></svg></a>
          <label class="bar-language">
            <span class="visually-hidden" id="language-label"></span>
            <svg class="bar-language__mark" viewBox="0 0 20 20" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M3 10a7 7 0 1 0 14 0a7 7 0 1 0 -14 0M10 3C7.4 5.3 7.4 14.7 10 17M10 3C12.6 5.3 12.6 14.7 10 17M3 10H17M4.2 6.2H15.8M4.2 13.8H15.8" vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke" /></svg>
            <span class="chooser__field">
              <select id="language" class="chooser__select"></select>
              <svg class="chooser__mark" viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M4 6.5 L8 10.5 L12 6.5" /></svg>
            </span>
          </label>
        </span>
      </header>

      <!--
        The drawing, in the page's own flow rather than fixed behind it. The
        words used to lie across it; they sit under it now, which is the
        arrangement `PlantDetailView` uses in the app. See site.css §An area
        page.
      -->
      <canvas class="walk-stage" id="stage" aria-label="The Knot Garden"></canvas>

      <!--
        Two panels under the drawing, side by side on a wide window and one
        above the other on a phone: what this area is — its name and what its
        plants mean, set as a dictionary entry — and the ways about it and out
        of it. See site.css §The two panels.
      -->
      <div class="area-panels">
      <section class="area-panel area-panel--words">
      <h1 class="heading" data-s="areaPattern"></h1>
      <p class="body walk-about visually-hidden" data-s="knotAbout"></p>

      <!--
        One line, and only when there is something to say: while the plants are
        being grown, or when there is nothing here yet. It carries no count — a
        running tally of plots and plants is a readout, and this is a garden.
      -->
      <p class="label walk-note" id="note" role="status" hidden></p>

      <!--
        What gathers here: the theme this area is, the syllables that bring a
        plant to it, and its three parts, with a way to the whole table. Written
        by `meanings.js` from the one table `/meanings` is drawn from, so the two
        cannot come to disagree. Hidden until it is written, so a page whose
        script has not run shows nothing rather than an empty band.
      -->
      <section class="gathers" id="gathers" hidden></section>

      </section>
      <div class="area-panel area-panel--ways walk-ways">
        <!--
          The pad: four directions, closer and further, and the two turns.
          Written by `movepad.js`, the same on every area page, so it is
          described there and in site.css §The pad rather than nine times
          here. Hidden until there is a plot to move about.
        -->
        <nav class="walk-keys" id="keys" role="group" hidden></nav>

        <!--
          The garden's map, small: ten cells five by two, this area filled, the
          other built areas lit and each a link to its page, the closed ones
          faint and inert. Drawn by `gates.js` from `garden.js`'s own table, so
          there is one map rather than a second one written out here.
        -->
        <nav class="minimap" id="minimap" hidden></nav>
      </div>
      </div>


      <!--
        The foot: one hairline and the privacy notice. The language it used to
        hold went up into the bar, beside the other ways on from a page.
      -->
      <footer class="foot bar-foot">
        <a class="bar-foot__link" href="/privacy" data-s="privacyTitle"></a>
      </footer>
    </main>

    <script type="module" src="/assets/js/plain.js"></script>
    <script type="module" src="/assets/js/knotpage.js"></script>
  </body>
</html>
